  "question_type": "CASESTUDY",
  "question": "**Case Study 13: Random Variable Distribution**  \nA die is thrown twice. Let X be the number of times a 6 appears.  \n(a) Find the probability distribution of X.  \n(b) Find the mean and variance of X.  \n(c) What is the probability X is at least 1?",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Possible values of X: 0,1,2<br />$P(X=0)$: No 6 in both = $(5/6)^2=25/36$<br />$P(X=1)$: 6 appears in exactly one throw = $2\\times(1/6)\\times(5/6)=10/36$ (When one is 6, other is not; 2 ways)<br />$P(X=2)$: Both 6 = $(1/6)^2=1/36$<br /><br />Distribution:<br />$X$: 0; 1; 2<br />$P(X)$: $25/36$; $10/36$; $1/36$<br /><br />Mean: $0\\times25/36+1\\times10/36+2\\times1/36=0+10/36+2/36=12/36=1/3$<br />Variance:<br />$E(X^2)=0\\times25/36+1\\times10/36+4\\times1/36=0+10/36+4/36=14/36$<br />$Var(X)=E(X^2)-(E(X))^2=14/36-(1/3)^2=14/36-1/9=14/36-4/36=10/36=5/18$<br /><br />(c) $P(X\\geq1)=P(X=1)+P(X=2)=10/36+1/36=11/36$<br /><br />**Answers:**<br />(a) See above<br />(b) Mean $1/3$, Variance $5/18$<br />(c) $11/36$",
